86560957 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d671a888-afe9-57c8-b3e6-45e7cda377e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & ∗ \\\\ 1 & b & ∗ \\\\ 1 & c & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $b-c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07e07602-03de-5f15-9936-edc2a5baccd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560971 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}+2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3}+2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}-3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3}-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}+2 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $b-c=2-0=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2858ac6f-cd8b-5d47-8d03-8e772f9be1a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560982 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=x,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1214d66-3e53-55a0-9e07-8e3f61632afe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561035 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(4)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =4,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(4) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(4) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-4I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-4I)^{2}z=(A-4I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c24f3edc-790e-54ee-a83e-334a474274a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561045 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =4,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(4) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to